me high is 109\u00b0F, on August 27, 2023.","t_chg":"Lake Charles' nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 113 complete years: Lake Charles' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.24\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.32), about +3.2\u00b0F since 1896. Lake Charles' average high shows no clear change: \u22120.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.09 to +0.04) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1910s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.85) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.58\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.33 to +0.84). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 5 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Lake Charles' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.32 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 59.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2002 (85.18 in); the driest, 1954 (30.08 in). Lake Charles almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.2 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}